    <title>Retrieval-Augmented Generation for
   Scientific Question Answering</title>
    <summary>  We present a study of retrieval-augmented
generation applied to scientific question answering over
a large corpus of papers. Our method reduces hallucination
by grounding answers in retrieved passages.
</summary>
